WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.375
In this lesson you'll learn how today's

2
00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:06.407
AI models work, and how much

3
00:00:06.407 --> 00:00:09.344
that picture has changed in five years:

4
00:00:09.344 --> 00:00:11.457
what they take in and make,

5
00:00:11.457 --> 00:00:14.545
how they remember, what they cost,

6
00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:17.041
and the many places you'll meet them.

7
00:00:17.041 --> 00:00:19.863
Underneath it all is still a model

8
00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:23.508
that only knows what's on its desk right now;

9
00:00:23.508 --> 00:00:26.132
nearly everything new since then

10
00:00:26.132 --> 00:00:28.907
is about what gets put on that desk,

11
00:00:28.907 --> 00:00:31.438
what it hands off to other models,

12
00:00:31.438 --> 00:00:33.586
and where the desk sits.

13
00:00:33.586 --> 00:00:36.477
Each topic gets a minute or two here;

14
00:00:36.477 --> 00:00:40.217
most get their own lesson later.

15
00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:43.928
On the course map, this lesson sits at the Start,

16
00:00:43.928 --> 00:00:46.773
in Orientation: what this course is,

17
00:00:46.773 --> 00:00:48.816
and the lantern you carry.

18
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:52.357
The lanterns strung over every district are AI.

19
00:00:52.357 --> 00:00:55.399
They aren't a district; they light all six.

20
00:00:55.399 --> 00:00:57.895
This lesson is a tour of the whole lantern.

21
00:00:57.895 --> 00:00:59.764
You'll know where each piece goes,

22
00:00:59.764 --> 00:01:01.610
not how to do it yet.

23
00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.433
Memory lands in The Code,

24
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:07.102
plugins and caching in The Network,

25
00:01:07.102 --> 00:01:11.016
work plans and personal agents in Trust.

26
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.185
The model is a brilliant clerk

27
00:01:13.185 --> 00:01:16.726
who knows only what is on the desk right now.

28
00:01:16.726 --> 00:01:19.733
Five years ago, in autumn twenty twenty-one,

29
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:21.312
the desk was tiny.

30
00:01:21.312 --> 00:01:24.645
Text cards only, about two thousand tokens' worth,

31
00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:26.990
and one letter slot in the door:

32
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.370
an API for programmers.

33
00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.528
No ChatGPT, no memory, no tools.

34
00:01:32.528 --> 00:01:34.548
Now the desk is the size of a hall,

35
00:01:34.548 --> 00:01:37.543
with photos and recordings on it, and rooms,

36
00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:40.250
phone lines and a picture studio around it.

37
00:01:40.713 --> 00:01:43.209
Two things did not change.

38
00:01:43.209 --> 00:01:46.251
The clerk still writes one small piece at a time,

39
00:01:46.251 --> 00:01:48.155
guessing what comes next.

40
00:01:48.155 --> 00:01:50.500
And nothing stays overnight.

41
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.147
Every request starts with a clean desk,

42
00:01:53.147 --> 00:01:56.307
and everything is handed over again.

43
00:01:56.756 --> 00:01:59.113
The desk is measured in tokens.

44
00:01:59.113 --> 00:02:01.330
A token is a piece of a word,

45
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.861
roughly four characters of English.

46
00:02:03.861 --> 00:02:07.228
You're limited and billed in tokens.

47
00:02:07.228 --> 00:02:09.527
Everything on the desk counts:

48
00:02:09.527 --> 00:02:11.919
the app's hidden instructions,

49
00:02:11.919 --> 00:02:15.158
every message so far, your files and images,

50
00:02:15.158 --> 00:02:16.876
the result of every tool,

51
00:02:16.876 --> 00:02:19.732
its thinking, and its reply.

52
00:02:19.732 --> 00:02:21.393
The desk grew fast.

53
00:02:21.393 --> 00:02:25.445
About two thousand tokens in twenty twenty.

54
00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:27.407
A hundred thousand on Claude

55
00:02:27.407 --> 00:02:29.218
in twenty twenty-three.

56
00:02:29.218 --> 00:02:32.190
A million on Gemini in twenty twenty-four,

57
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.605
and today a million is standard

58
00:02:34.605 --> 00:02:36.498
on Claude's bigger models.

59
00:02:36.498 --> 00:02:38.437
You'll see it as a meter:

60
00:02:38.437 --> 00:02:42.175
context used, context left.

61
00:02:42.175 --> 00:02:43.452
One catch:

62
00:02:43.452 --> 00:02:46.633
token counts don't compare across models.

63
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.525
Newer Claude models can count up

64
00:02:48.525 --> 00:02:51.265
to a third more tokens for the same text.

65
00:02:51.265 --> 00:02:54.156
Here the picture breaks: no clerk stays.

66
00:02:54.156 --> 00:02:56.130
Each reply is a fresh copy

67
00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.964
that reads the whole desk at once.

68
00:02:59.414 --> 00:03:01.875
A fuller desk is a worse desk.

69
00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:04.940
As it fills, the clerk gets worse,

70
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.995
well before it's full.

71
00:03:06.995 --> 00:03:09.422
Researchers at Chroma measured it across

72
00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:13.032
eighteen models in twenty twenty-five:

73
00:03:13.032 --> 00:03:14.716
context rot.

74
00:03:14.716 --> 00:03:16.272
Things buried in the middle

75
00:03:16.272 --> 00:03:18.977
of a long pile get missed most.

76
00:03:18.977 --> 00:03:21.961
So tools now tidy the desk.

77
00:03:21.961 --> 00:03:23.900
Compaction summarises the pile

78
00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.396
onto one page and carries on.

79
00:03:26.396 --> 00:03:28.242
Claude Code does it by itself,

80
00:03:28.242 --> 00:03:32.886
as auto-compact, or when you type /compact.

81
00:03:32.886 --> 00:03:35.986
Old tool results get swept off.

82
00:03:35.986 --> 00:03:38.807
A sub-agent gets its own fresh desk

83
00:03:38.807 --> 00:03:42.894
for a side job, and hands back a short report.

84
00:03:42.894 --> 00:03:45.007
Prompt engineering has grown

85
00:03:45.007 --> 00:03:47.341
into context engineering:

86
00:03:47.341 --> 00:03:49.361
choosing what goes on the desk.

87
00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:53.635
It gets its own lesson in The Machine.

88
00:03:54.084 --> 00:03:57.149
Five years ago, the desk held only text.

89
00:03:57.149 --> 00:03:58.670
Now photos, PDFs,

90
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.038
recordings and video can land on it.

91
00:04:01.038 --> 00:04:02.571
That's multimodal,

92
00:04:02.571 --> 00:04:05.264
and the word hides three different things.

93
00:04:05.264 --> 00:04:08.921
One: the model reads many kinds of input.

94
00:04:08.921 --> 00:04:13.066
Claude reads text, images and PDFs.

95
00:04:13.066 --> 00:04:15.295
Gemini takes text, images,

96
00:04:15.295 --> 00:04:17.814
audio and video in one request.

97
00:04:17.814 --> 00:04:21.541
Two: the model makes pictures or sound itself.

98
00:04:21.541 --> 00:04:22.737
That's rarer.

99
00:04:22.737 --> 00:04:24.212
Three, the most common:

100
00:04:24.212 --> 00:04:26.313
the app hands the job to a separate

101
00:04:26.313 --> 00:04:30.818
specialist model, through an API or a tool.

102
00:04:30.818 --> 00:04:33.511
The clerk phones the studio next door.

103
00:04:33.511 --> 00:04:35.787
Claude writes only text.

104
00:04:35.787 --> 00:04:37.180
For a chart or a picture,

105
00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.223
it writes code that draws one,

106
00:04:39.223 --> 00:04:42.173
or calls another model through a connector.

107
00:04:42.173 --> 00:04:44.982
ChatGPT feels like one model,

108
00:04:44.982 --> 00:04:47.142
but it stitches several together:

109
00:04:47.142 --> 00:04:49.557
one for images, one for voice.

110
00:04:49.557 --> 00:04:51.600
And OpenAI shut down its Sora

111
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.620
video app this spring.

112
00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.547
You'll meet all this as a paperclip

113
00:04:55.547 --> 00:04:58.926
that accepts some files and refuses others.

114
00:04:58.926 --> 00:05:02.098
Its own lesson lives in The Network.

115
00:05:02.547 --> 00:05:04.672
Most of those studios run a different

116
00:05:04.672 --> 00:05:07.121
kind of model, called diffusion.

117
00:05:07.121 --> 00:05:10.024
Picture a page of pure static.

118
00:05:10.024 --> 00:05:13.275
The model wipes away a little of the noise,

119
00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:17.698
step by step, each step nudged by your prompt,

120
00:05:17.698 --> 00:05:19.799
until a picture is left.

121
00:05:19.799 --> 00:05:21.634
The same trick makes images,

122
00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:23.863
video, sound effects and music,

123
00:05:23.863 --> 00:05:26.208
as far as their makers have said.

124
00:05:26.208 --> 00:05:28.693
Voices mostly aren't.

125
00:05:28.693 --> 00:05:31.514
Most current ones use a close cousin called

126
00:05:31.514 --> 00:05:34.834
flow matching, which needs fewer steps.

127
00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.992
Not every image model is diffusion, though.

128
00:05:37.992 --> 00:05:41.196
OpenAI's image models are reported to build

129
00:05:41.196 --> 00:05:44.784
pictures piece by piece, like text.

130
00:05:44.784 --> 00:05:48.464
And diffusion can write text, rarely.

131
00:05:48.464 --> 00:05:51.262
Inception's Mercury and Google's experimental

132
00:05:51.262 --> 00:05:54.618
diffusion models start from a smudged page

133
00:05:54.618 --> 00:05:56.940
and sharpen every word at once.

134
00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.214
Very fast, but not yet frontier quality.

135
00:06:00.214 --> 00:06:02.663
Every top chat model still writes

136
00:06:02.663 --> 00:06:05.090
one token after another.

137
00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.273
How generators work gets its own

138
00:06:07.273 --> 00:06:09.538
lesson in The Machine.

139
00:06:09.989 --> 00:06:13.089
The lamp over the desk used to have dials.

140
00:06:13.089 --> 00:06:15.353
Temperature: how much randomness

141
00:06:15.353 --> 00:06:17.059
goes into each guess.

142
00:06:17.059 --> 00:06:20.333
Top P and top K: how many candidate

143
00:06:20.333 --> 00:06:22.679
words the clerk may choose from.

144
00:06:22.679 --> 00:06:24.803
Max output tokens:

145
00:06:24.803 --> 00:06:28.123
a hard cap on the length of the reply.

146
00:06:28.123 --> 00:06:30.295
This year, the newest Claude models

147
00:06:30.295 --> 00:06:33.151
stopped accepting a custom temperature.

148
00:06:33.151 --> 00:06:35.728
Send one, and you get an error.

149
00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:39.408
The dial that matters now is effort, or thinking:

150
00:06:39.408 --> 00:06:42.682
how long the clerk works before it answers,

151
00:06:42.682 --> 00:06:45.422
from low up to max.

152
00:06:45.422 --> 00:06:49.067
And the clerk can still be confidently wrong.

153
00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:53.177
Less often with search and tools, but not never.

154
00:06:53.177 --> 00:06:55.407
Now it can also take a wrong action,

155
00:06:55.407 --> 00:06:58.727
or invent a package that doesn't exist.

156
00:06:58.727 --> 00:07:02.537
The settings get their own lesson in The Network.

157
00:07:02.987 --> 00:07:04.891
The clerk keeps nothing,

158
00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:09.013
so every memory is paper put back on the desk.

159
00:07:09.013 --> 00:07:11.486
There are five ways it comes back.

160
00:07:11.486 --> 00:07:14.225
One: the conversation itself.

161
00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:18.231
The app re-sends every message, every turn.

162
00:07:18.231 --> 00:07:21.725
Two: a memory the assistant keeps about you,

163
00:07:21.725 --> 00:07:24.140
a profile it writes itself.

164
00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.658
Claude, ChatGPT and Gemini all have one,

165
00:07:27.658 --> 00:07:30.734
and on Claude it's no longer a paid extra.

166
00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:33.765
Three: searching old chats with a tool.

167
00:07:33.765 --> 00:07:37.666
You'll see a line like: searched past chats.

168
00:07:37.666 --> 00:07:39.906
Four: stores outside the app,

169
00:07:39.906 --> 00:07:41.706
reached by tool calls.

170
00:07:41.706 --> 00:07:43.412
Memory files on your computer,

171
00:07:43.412 --> 00:07:46.466
which Claude Code keeps, or a page in Notion

172
00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:49.159
or Google Drive, through a connector.

173
00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:50.669
Five: RAG,

174
00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:53.594
short for retrieval augmented generation,

175
00:07:53.594 --> 00:07:56.450
the general name for what three and four do.

176
00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.250
Fetch the relevant pieces,

177
00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:00.908
put them on the desk, then answer.

178
00:08:00.908 --> 00:08:03.253
Vector search is one way to fetch;

179
00:08:03.253 --> 00:08:05.541
coding agents now mostly search

180
00:08:05.541 --> 00:08:07.468
with plain file tools.

181
00:08:07.468 --> 00:08:12.460
Each one costs a tool call, desk space, or both.

182
00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.990
Memory and RAG get their own lessons in The Code.

183
00:08:17.441 --> 00:08:20.645
Some paper sits on the desk every turn.

184
00:08:20.645 --> 00:08:22.654
First, the system prompt:

185
00:08:22.654 --> 00:08:25.371
the app's own hidden instructions,

186
00:08:25.371 --> 00:08:28.041
read before anything you type.

187
00:08:28.041 --> 00:08:31.675
Anthropic publishes the ones for its Claude apps.

188
00:08:31.675 --> 00:08:33.707
Second, your standing instructions:

189
00:08:33.707 --> 00:08:36.737
preferences, styles and project instructions,

190
00:08:36.737 --> 00:08:39.686
set once and re-sent every turn.

191
00:08:39.686 --> 00:08:43.308
Third, instruction files that load themselves.

192
00:08:43.308 --> 00:08:46.849
Put a file called CLAUDE.md at the root

193
00:08:46.849 --> 00:08:49.275
of a project, and Claude Code reads

194
00:08:49.275 --> 00:08:51.911
it at the start of every session there.

195
00:08:51.911 --> 00:08:53.745
Your user folder, your company

196
00:08:53.745 --> 00:08:56.369
and sub-folders can have one too.

197
00:08:56.369 --> 00:09:00.885
AGENTS.md is the open version other tools use,

198
00:09:00.885 --> 00:09:03.196
and Claude Code now reads it too,

199
00:09:03.196 --> 00:09:05.670
when there's no CLAUDE.md.

200
00:09:06.133 --> 00:09:10.997
GEMINI.md and Cursor's rules are the same idea.

201
00:09:10.997 --> 00:09:12.843
But a note is not a lock.

202
00:09:12.843 --> 00:09:15.583
Hooks and permissions enforce.

203
00:09:15.583 --> 00:09:18.265
A long note gets followed less,

204
00:09:18.265 --> 00:09:21.237
and it costs tokens every turn.

205
00:09:21.237 --> 00:09:22.967
Instruction files get their own

206
00:09:22.967 --> 00:09:25.082
lesson in The Machine.

207
00:09:25.532 --> 00:09:29.236
Re-sending the whole desk is slow and expensive,

208
00:09:29.236 --> 00:09:31.523
so providers keep it warm.

209
00:09:31.523 --> 00:09:34.472
With prompt caching, the provider stores the work

210
00:09:34.472 --> 00:09:37.316
it did reading the start of your request,

211
00:09:37.316 --> 00:09:40.521
the prefix, and reuses it when the next

212
00:09:40.521 --> 00:09:43.470
request starts exactly the same way.

213
00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.873
Order matters: tools first,

214
00:09:45.873 --> 00:09:48.868
then the system prompt, then the messages.

215
00:09:48.868 --> 00:09:51.295
Change anything near the top, and everything

216
00:09:51.295 --> 00:09:54.035
below it is laid out again from scratch.

217
00:09:54.035 --> 00:09:58.377
The layout sits under a timer, its time to live.

218
00:09:58.377 --> 00:10:00.118
Five minutes by default,

219
00:10:00.118 --> 00:10:02.243
or an hour at a higher price,

220
00:10:02.243 --> 00:10:05.807
and every reuse resets the clock.

221
00:10:05.807 --> 00:10:09.325
Claude Code keeps it an hour on a subscription,

222
00:10:09.325 --> 00:10:12.193
and five minutes on an API key.

223
00:10:12.193 --> 00:10:13.470
Take a longer break,

224
00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:17.220
and your next message rebuilds the whole desk.

225
00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.030
Other things break it too: switching models,

226
00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.387
changing tools, dropping old images,

227
00:10:22.387 --> 00:10:26.603
compaction, and on older models, effort.

228
00:10:27.367 --> 00:10:30.212
Editing CLAUDE.md doesn't;

229
00:10:30.212 --> 00:10:33.416
the edit waits for your next session.

230
00:10:33.416 --> 00:10:35.703
The cache isn't the clerk remembering.

231
00:10:35.703 --> 00:10:37.897
It's stored arithmetic,

232
00:10:37.897 --> 00:10:40.929
thrown away when the timer runs out.

233
00:10:41.379 --> 00:10:43.678
That's why the bill has four meters.

234
00:10:43.678 --> 00:10:46.940
Normal input: fresh paper, full price.

235
00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:51.236
Cache write: laying the desk out for reuse,

236
00:10:51.236 --> 00:10:53.233
at a little more than input.

237
00:10:53.233 --> 00:10:56.565
On Claude, a quarter more for five minutes,

238
00:10:56.565 --> 00:10:58.329
double for an hour.

239
00:10:58.329 --> 00:11:01.336
Cache read: reusing the layout,

240
00:11:01.336 --> 00:11:04.007
at a tenth of the input price, or less.

241
00:11:04.007 --> 00:11:06.665
Output: everything the clerk writes,

242
00:11:06.665 --> 00:11:08.581
its thinking included.

243
00:11:08.581 --> 00:11:10.137
That's the expensive meter,

244
00:11:10.137 --> 00:11:12.830
about five times the input price.

245
00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:16.441
The API reports these four counts.

246
00:11:16.441 --> 00:11:19.587
So a long chat costs more every turn,

247
00:11:19.587 --> 00:11:21.224
even if you type one word,

248
00:11:21.224 --> 00:11:23.755
because the whole desk is sent again.

249
00:11:23.755 --> 00:11:26.971
It's cheap only while the cache is warm.

250
00:11:26.971 --> 00:11:30.257
And cached tokens are cheaper, not smaller.

251
00:11:30.257 --> 00:11:32.358
They still fill the desk.

252
00:11:32.358 --> 00:11:35.818
OpenAI and Google cache automatically;

253
00:11:35.818 --> 00:11:40.311
on Claude's API, you switch it on.

254
00:11:40.311 --> 00:11:43.912
The meters get their own lesson in The Network.

255
00:11:44.362 --> 00:11:48.100
The same clerk works in many rooms and buildings.

256
00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:50.167
A mode is the kind of job.

257
00:11:50.167 --> 00:11:52.872
A platform is where it runs,

258
00:11:52.872 --> 00:11:54.753
and what it can touch.

259
00:11:54.753 --> 00:11:59.083
Claude's modes were chat, Cowork and Code.

260
00:11:59.083 --> 00:12:01.510
Cowork, launched in January,

261
00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:05.156
is an agent for office work on your files.

262
00:12:05.156 --> 00:12:08.616
In mid-September, Anthropic began merging chat

263
00:12:08.616 --> 00:12:12.017
and Cowork into one Claude that decides

264
00:12:12.017 --> 00:12:14.699
whether to answer you or do the work.

265
00:12:14.699 --> 00:12:18.438
It's rolling out to Pro and Max first.

266
00:12:18.438 --> 00:12:21.190
Claude Code stays its own room.

267
00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.150
The buildings: the browser, the desktop app,

268
00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.913
your phone, the terminal CLI,

269
00:12:26.913 --> 00:12:30.095
editor extensions, and cloud sessions

270
00:12:30.095 --> 00:12:32.788
that run on Anthropic's machines.

271
00:12:32.788 --> 00:12:34.100
Local sees your files,

272
00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:36.492
and stops when your laptop sleeps.

273
00:12:36.492 --> 00:12:38.546
Cloud sees only what you give it,

274
00:12:38.546 --> 00:12:40.789
and keeps going with the lid shut.

275
00:12:41.252 --> 00:12:42.796
OpenAI pulled Chat,

276
00:12:42.796 --> 00:12:46.813
Work and Codex into one desktop app in July.

277
00:12:46.813 --> 00:12:51.737
Google folded Gemini CLI into Antigravity in June.

278
00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.976
Coding agents live in The Machine.

279
00:12:55.426 --> 00:12:57.922
A skill is a folder of instructions:

280
00:12:57.922 --> 00:13:02.972
a file called SKILL.md, and sometimes scripts.

281
00:13:02.972 --> 00:13:05.213
Only its label sits on the desk;

282
00:13:05.213 --> 00:13:08.174
the pages load when the clerk needs them.

283
00:13:08.174 --> 00:13:12.853
It's an open format other tools now use too.

284
00:13:12.853 --> 00:13:15.709
A connector is a phone line to another service,

285
00:13:15.709 --> 00:13:20.933
like Gmail, Notion or GitHub, built on MCP.

286
00:13:20.933 --> 00:13:23.975
A plugin is a boxed kit: skills,

287
00:13:23.975 --> 00:13:26.645
connectors, commands and hooks, together.

288
00:13:26.645 --> 00:13:29.060
You get them from the app's directory,

289
00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:31.278
the Claude Marketplace,

290
00:13:31.278 --> 00:13:33.751
reported at over two thousand add-ons,

291
00:13:33.751 --> 00:13:36.990
and Claude Code plugin marketplaces,

292
00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.790
which are git repositories.

293
00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:41.146
OpenAI has its own too.

294
00:13:41.146 --> 00:13:44.989
Every third-party add-on is code you're trusting.

295
00:13:44.989 --> 00:13:47.392
OpenClaw's skill store held hundreds

296
00:13:47.392 --> 00:13:50.515
of malicious ones this February.

297
00:13:50.515 --> 00:13:52.897
All three live in The Network.

298
00:13:53.347 --> 00:13:56.424
Same clerk, different building rules.

299
00:13:56.424 --> 00:14:00.812
Individual plans, Free, Pro and Max,

300
00:14:00.812 --> 00:14:03.459
mostly differ in how much you can use.

301
00:14:03.459 --> 00:14:05.828
And since autumn twenty twenty-five,

302
00:14:05.828 --> 00:14:09.078
consumer chats, paid ones included,

303
00:14:09.078 --> 00:14:12.654
may be used for training unless you turn that off.

304
00:14:12.654 --> 00:14:16.195
Team and Enterprise add the company building:

305
00:14:16.195 --> 00:14:18.169
single sign-on at the door,

306
00:14:18.169 --> 00:14:21.025
admins choosing which connectors,

307
00:14:21.025 --> 00:14:23.509
plugins and skills are allowed,

308
00:14:23.509 --> 00:14:26.679
audit logs, custom data retention,

309
00:14:26.679 --> 00:14:29.709
and instruction files pushed to every machine.

310
00:14:29.709 --> 00:14:32.101
Work plans aren't trained on by default,

311
00:14:32.101 --> 00:14:36.083
and admins get notice before products change.

312
00:14:36.083 --> 00:14:36.954
Since April,

313
00:14:36.954 --> 00:14:39.624
a Claude subscription's included usage can't

314
00:14:39.624 --> 00:14:42.713
power third-party agents like OpenClaw.

315
00:14:42.713 --> 00:14:44.245
Those are billed separately,

316
00:14:44.245 --> 00:14:47.090
by usage or an API key.

317
00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:51.236
Plans at work get their own lesson in Trust.

318
00:14:51.686 --> 00:14:53.787
Claude Design launched in April

319
00:14:53.787 --> 00:14:57.433
as a research preview: describe a visual,

320
00:14:57.433 --> 00:15:00.417
and Claude drafts it as layout and code,

321
00:15:00.417 --> 00:15:02.460
not with an image model.

322
00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:06.083
Since September it lives inside chats too,

323
00:15:06.083 --> 00:15:09.450
now in beta, next to Claude Docs and Slides.

324
00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:10.495
And voice.

325
00:15:10.495 --> 00:15:11.807
It's built two ways.

326
00:15:11.807 --> 00:15:14.500
A pipeline: speech to text,

327
00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.822
then the model, then text to speech.

328
00:15:16.822 --> 00:15:18.366
It takes turns.

329
00:15:18.366 --> 00:15:21.083
Or a model that hears and speaks sound directly,

330
00:15:21.083 --> 00:15:24.415
catching your tone, and listens while it talks,

331
00:15:24.415 --> 00:15:25.984
so you can cut in.

332
00:15:26.435 --> 00:15:28.699
OpenAI's GPT Live

333
00:15:28.699 --> 00:15:31.776
and Google's Gemini Live work that way.

334
00:15:31.776 --> 00:15:34.434
Claude's voice mode can now use the bigger

335
00:15:34.434 --> 00:15:37.163
models and your connected apps.

336
00:15:37.163 --> 00:15:38.428
It takes turns,

337
00:15:38.428 --> 00:15:41.377
and Anthropic hasn't said how it's built.

338
00:15:41.377 --> 00:15:43.885
Builders get a lesson in Shipping;

339
00:15:43.885 --> 00:15:46.696
voice, in The Network.

340
00:15:47.146 --> 00:15:49.723
Last, a desk that never closes.

341
00:15:49.723 --> 00:15:52.556
A personal agent runs all the time,

342
00:15:52.556 --> 00:15:54.321
on its own computer.

343
00:15:54.321 --> 00:15:56.225
You message it from a chat app,

344
00:15:56.225 --> 00:15:57.839
it keeps a long memory,

345
00:15:57.839 --> 00:16:00.741
and it has broad permission to act.

346
00:16:00.741 --> 00:16:04.166
One camp is open source and self-hosted.

347
00:16:04.166 --> 00:16:06.616
OpenClaw started as Clawdbot,

348
00:16:06.616 --> 00:16:08.996
and went viral in January.

349
00:16:08.996 --> 00:16:10.505
Hermes Agent,

350
00:16:10.505 --> 00:16:13.559
a rival from Nous Research, followed in February,

351
00:16:13.559 --> 00:16:17.169
and turns problems it solves into its own skills.

352
00:16:17.169 --> 00:16:20.699
Your machine, your keys, your risk.

353
00:16:20.699 --> 00:16:23.648
In February, OpenClaw had its crisis:

354
00:16:23.648 --> 00:16:25.169
more than a hundred thousand

355
00:16:25.169 --> 00:16:27.537
installs left open to the internet.

356
00:16:27.537 --> 00:16:30.916
The other camp is hosted by a big company.

357
00:16:30.916 --> 00:16:35.153
Meta's Muse launched in September, in the US.

358
00:16:35.153 --> 00:16:38.857
OpenAI announced dots at the end of September,

359
00:16:38.857 --> 00:16:40.657
rolling out now.

360
00:16:40.657 --> 00:16:44.801
Google has Gemini Spark, on its paid AI plans.

361
00:16:44.801 --> 00:16:47.971
Their computer, their approval rules.

362
00:16:47.971 --> 00:16:51.512
Anthropic's nearest pieces are cloud sessions,

363
00:16:51.512 --> 00:16:54.171
scheduled routines, and Dispatch,

364
00:16:54.171 --> 00:16:56.759
which sends a task from your phone.

365
00:16:56.759 --> 00:16:59.198
Their own lesson is in Trust.

366
00:16:59.198 --> 00:17:01.752
So what's myth, and what's still true?

367
00:17:01.752 --> 00:17:03.286
Here's June.

368
00:17:03.736 --> 00:17:06.302
Thanks. Ten myths, fast.

369
00:17:06.302 --> 00:17:08.612
Myth one: set temperature to zero,

370
00:17:08.612 --> 00:17:10.725
and you get the same answer every time.

371
00:17:10.725 --> 00:17:11.933
Wrong twice.

372
00:17:11.933 --> 00:17:13.604
The newest Claude models return

373
00:17:13.604 --> 00:17:15.114
an error if you set it,

374
00:17:15.114 --> 00:17:18.341
and zero never guaranteed the same answer anyway.

375
00:17:18.341 --> 00:17:21.569
Myth two: it remembers me. Not quite.

376
00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:23.786
It re-reads a profile it wrote,

377
00:17:23.786 --> 00:17:26.480
searches your old chats, or opens a file.

378
00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.420
Nothing lives inside the model.

379
00:17:29.022 --> 00:17:30.183
Myth three:

380
00:17:30.183 --> 00:17:32.947
a bigger window means it remembers everything.

381
00:17:32.947 --> 00:17:35.628
No. A fuller desk is a worse desk.

382
00:17:35.628 --> 00:17:38.867
Myth four: all AI art is diffusion.

383
00:17:38.867 --> 00:17:40.133
Mostly, not all.

384
00:17:40.133 --> 00:17:41.897
Some image models build pictures

385
00:17:41.897 --> 00:17:43.813
piece by piece, like text.

386
00:17:43.813 --> 00:17:46.263
Myth five: Claude can draw that for you.

387
00:17:46.263 --> 00:17:47.714
It writes code that draws,

388
00:17:47.714 --> 00:17:49.421
or it calls another model.

389
00:17:49.421 --> 00:17:51.965
The paintbrush belongs to someone else.

390
00:17:52.415 --> 00:17:57.163
Five more. Myth six: cached tokens are free.

391
00:17:57.163 --> 00:18:00.194
Writing the cache costs more than normal input.

392
00:18:00.194 --> 00:18:01.273
Reading it is cheap,

393
00:18:01.273 --> 00:18:03.514
but those tokens still fill the desk.

394
00:18:03.514 --> 00:18:05.848
And a coffee break longer than the timer means

395
00:18:05.848 --> 00:18:08.507
your next message rebuilds from scratch.

396
00:18:08.507 --> 00:18:10.922
Myth seven: I pay for Pro,

397
00:18:10.922 --> 00:18:13.592
so my chats aren't used for training.

398
00:18:13.592 --> 00:18:16.727
Consumer plans may be, unless you switch it off.

399
00:18:16.727 --> 00:18:19.037
Work plans aren't, by default.

400
00:18:19.037 --> 00:18:22.671
Myth eight: my subscription can run any agent.

401
00:18:22.671 --> 00:18:25.515
Not on your plan's usage, not since April.

402
00:18:25.515 --> 00:18:27.326
Those are billed separately.

403
00:18:27.326 --> 00:18:31.401
Myth nine: CLAUDE.md is a rule it must obey.

404
00:18:31.401 --> 00:18:33.003
It's a note on the desk.

405
00:18:33.003 --> 00:18:35.035
Hooks and permissions are the locks.

406
00:18:35.035 --> 00:18:38.657
Myth ten: Sora is where AI video lives.

407
00:18:38.657 --> 00:18:40.933
OpenAI shut that app this spring;

408
00:18:40.933 --> 00:18:42.674
the other studios carry on.

409
00:18:42.674 --> 00:18:45.103
That's the round. Back to you.

410
00:18:45.553 --> 00:18:47.376
Thanks, June.

411
00:18:47.376 --> 00:18:50.847
Where is this heading, going by this year's moves?

412
00:18:50.847 --> 00:18:53.355
Fewer modes: one agent

413
00:18:53.355 --> 00:18:56.791
that decides whether to answer or to work.

414
00:18:56.791 --> 00:18:58.521
Agents that keep running on their own

415
00:18:58.521 --> 00:19:00.483
computers in the cloud.

416
00:19:00.483 --> 00:19:02.492
And curating the desk,

417
00:19:02.492 --> 00:19:04.988
instead of only making it bigger.

418
00:19:04.988 --> 00:19:07.463
Beyond that, nobody knows yet.

419
00:19:07.926 --> 00:19:10.155
Safe to ignore for now:

420
00:19:10.155 --> 00:19:12.245
version numbers, leaderboards,

421
00:19:12.245 --> 00:19:14.694
and the maths inside the model.

422
00:19:14.694 --> 00:19:16.656
Three things to keep.

423
00:19:16.656 --> 00:19:20.385
The model only knows what's on its desk right now.

424
00:19:20.848 --> 00:19:22.728
Memory and instruction files

425
00:19:22.728 --> 00:19:25.189
are text put back on the desk,

426
00:19:25.189 --> 00:19:28.080
and a fuller desk is a worse desk.

427
00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.994
Making pictures is mostly a hand-off.

428
00:19:30.994 --> 00:19:33.374
It reads many kinds of input,

429
00:19:33.374 --> 00:19:34.791
but pictures, video

430
00:19:34.791 --> 00:19:38.146
and music usually come from a separate model,

431
00:19:38.146 --> 00:19:40.468
most often diffusion.

432
00:19:40.468 --> 00:19:44.149
Same model, many rooms, four meters.

433
00:19:44.149 --> 00:19:47.040
Where you meet it changes what it can touch

434
00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:48.514
and what it costs,

435
00:19:48.514 --> 00:19:51.616
and a warm cache makes repeats cheap.
